/-!
# Vanishing above the dimension of the closed fibre, over a Noetherian local base

Let `A` be a Noetherian local ring, let `f : X ⟶ Spec A` be proper and suppose the fibre over the
closed point has topological Krull dimension at most `d`. Then

`Hᵖ(X, ℱ) = 0` for every `p > d` and every quasi-coherent `ℱ` on `X`.

This is the conclusion of [Stacks, Tag 02V7] at the closed point of the spectrum of a local ring,
reached by the route `IdealMultiplesCohomology.lean` and `IdealQuotientAcyclicity.lean` set up —
Nakayama's lemma applied to `Hᵖ(X, ℱ) = 𝔪 · Hᵖ(X, ℱ)` — rather than by the theorem on formal
functions ([Stacks, Tag 02OD]), which this development does not have.

## Main results

All in the namespace `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.TopDegreeBaseChange`.

* `eq_zero_of_top_le_smul_map_maximalIdeal`: **Nakayama's lemma across a ring map.** For
  `φ : A →+* R` with `A` local, a module over `R` which is finite over `A` and which is its own
  multiple by the `R`-ideal generated by `𝔪` is zero.
* `isQuasicoherent_kernel_factorThruImage_idealMultiples`: the auxiliary kernel of
  `epi_map_rightDerived_idealMultiples` is quasi-coherent.
* `isZero_rightDerived_of_forall_isCoherent`: the coherent-to-quasi-coherent transfer of
  `FinitePresentationSuffices.lean` in the `ℕ`-indexed spelling.
* `isZero_rightDerived_of_isZero_cokernel_baseRing`: the Nakayama step of
  `IdealMultiplesCohomology.lean` with its two side conditions read over the base ring `A`
  instead of over `Γ(X, ⊤)`.
* `isZero_rightDerived_of_closedFibre_dim_le` and
  `isZero_rightDerivedPlus_of_closedFibre_dim_le`: **the vanishing itself**, in the `ℕ`- and
  `ℤ`-indexed spellings.
* `isZero_rightDerivedPlus_of_closedFibre_dim_le_one`: the case `d = 1`, which is what
  [Stacks, Tag 0B4X] consumes.

## Implementation notes

**Nakayama is run over `A`, not over `Γ(X, ⊤)`.** `isZero_rightDerived_of_isZero_cokernel` asks
for `Module.Finite ↥Γ(X, ⊤) Hᵖ` and for the ideal to lie in the Jacobson radical of `Γ(X, ⊤)`.
Neither is available: the finiteness this development proves is [Stacks, Tag 02O6],
`AlgebraicGeometry.ProperCoherenceWitness.moduleFinite_cohomology`, which lands over the base
ring, and the Jacobson condition over `Γ(X, ⊤)` would need `Γ(X, ⊤)` to be module-finite over `A`
— that is [Stacks, Tag 02O6] in degree zero *together with* an identification of `R⁰Γ` with `Γ`,
which is absent. Over `A` both conditions are free: the finiteness is the tag, and `A` being
local makes `𝔪 = (0 : Ideal A).jacobson` (`IsLocalRing.jacobson_eq_maximalIdeal`).

The glue is `eq_zero_of_top_le_smul_map_maximalIdeal`. The `Γ(X, ⊤)`-submodule
`(𝔪 · Γ(X, ⊤)) · M` and the `A`-submodule `𝔪 · M` have the same elements, because
`r · φ(a) · m = a · (r · m)`; only one inclusion is needed, and it is proved by exhibiting
`𝔪 · M` as a `Γ(X, ⊤)`-submodule — `c · (a · m) = a · (c · m)`, the step that needs `Γ(X, ⊤)`
commutative — and then running `Submodule.span_induction` over the generators `φ '' 𝔪` of
`Ideal.map φ 𝔪`.

**The degree induction descends.** `epi_map_rightDerived_idealMultiples` needs `Hᵖ⁺¹` to vanish
on the kernel of `⨁_{a ∈ I} ℱ ⟶ Iℱ`, so the statement in degree `p` rests on the statement in
degree `p + 1`. The induction is well-founded because
`AlgebraicGeometry.exists_isZero_rightDerived_moduleGlobalSections_of_isProper` produces a single
degree `N` beyond which `Hᵖ` kills every quasi-coherent module on a scheme proper over an affine
base — with no Noetherian and no dimension hypothesis — so `∀ k q, N ≤ q + k → d < q → …` may be
proved by induction on `k`.

**The induction runs over quasi-coherent modules, the Nakayama step over coherent ones.** The
auxiliary kernel is quasi-coherent but never coherent, its ambient coproduct being indexed by the
whole of `𝔪 · Γ(X, ⊤)`; Nakayama, on the other hand, needs the finiteness of the cohomology,
which [Stacks, Tag 02O6] gives only for a coherent module. Each step of the induction therefore
first restricts to the coherent modules, by
`isZero_rightDerivedPlus_moduleGlobalSections_of_forall_isCoherent` of
`FinitePresentationSuffices.lean`, and only then applies Nakayama; the hypothesis it feeds back
into the next degree is about quasi-coherent modules again.

**[Stacks, Tag 02V7] is not registered here.** The tag has `Y` locally Noetherian, `y` an
arbitrary point of `Y`, and concludes `(Rᵖ f_* ℱ)_y = 0`. What is proved here has `Y` the
spectrum of a Noetherian *local* ring and `y` its closed point — a case of the tag, not the tag.
The conclusion is in exchange stated for *quasi-coherent* rather than coherent `ℱ`, which is
stronger than the tag asks, and for every `d` rather than only `d = 1`.

**The local hypothesis is not needed, and no flat base change replaces it.**
`NoetherianBaseVanishing.lean` proves the same vanishing over an arbitrary Noetherian affine base
with *every* fibre bounded, by running the Nakayama step below at every maximal ideal of the base
ring at once; the fibre over a maximal ideal is closed whether or not the ring is local, which is
the only thing the support bound of `IdealQuotientAcyclicity.lean` asks of it. The statements here
are kept because they are what the one-dimensional-local development consumes, and because the
bound they ask for is only at the closed fibre.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 02V7](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02V7)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 02O6](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02O6)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01PK](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01PK)
-/

